**Rule (Independent evidence)** · `rule:design:independence` Establish completeness with an inventory oracle, algebra with independently written small-case calculations, numerical containment with a separately reviewed directed method, and encoding with independently authored bytes. Record shared dependencies so that correlated evidence is visible. The dense reference evaluator must not call the sparse production compiler to obtain its expected coefficients. A replay match remains useful integration evidence but cannot discharge all of these duties.

**Rule (Evidence statuses)** · `rule:design:evidence-status` A claim is planned, held, failed or passing with an execution artifact and its premises. Promotion to passing requires the exact supported input surface, profile and independent check to be named. Skipped, incompatible and unexecuted cases remain visible and never contribute to a passing total. Later audit and commissioning report this supported surface rather than inferring readiness from repository cleanliness.

## Resources and performance · `sec:design:resources`

**Experiment (Declared workloads)** · `expt:design:workloads` Each implementation bite declares workloads by anchored Actors, accepted acts, complete projections, dependency incidence, sparse base nonzeros, supported horizon, integer bit length, precision and raw path-prefix count. Include isolated Actors, dense support, shared dependency ancestors, equal-width differently shaped graphs, depleted relays and adversarial signed cycles. Record input construction separately from solve, publication and replay; no measured throughput or deployment capacity is claimed here.

**Requirement (Limit declaration)** · `req:design:limits` A supported execution profile supplies finite limits for input bytes, object count, references, nesting, rational bits, matrix support, horizon, interval precision, primitive operations, iterations, witness bytes and raw prefixes. Preflight excess reports unsupported; exhaustion during certified refinement reports INDECISIVE with its counter, while incomplete structural enumeration reports resource exhaustion without a mathematical verdict. A host's local deadline cannot rewrite the published certification policy.

**Rule (Cost accounting)** · `rule:design:costs` Sparse support propagation charges work in horizon times base nonzeros, including construction of contributing extrema and retained diagonals; fence evaluation is then a separate scalar cost. A map application charges sparse hop products and precision-dependent arithmetic. Factorized publication avoids a dense expansion but must retain the complete emitted operator. Raw enumeration charges visited-set work and every depth-dependent prefix, including parallel projections; it has no general constant-factor traversal claim (``prop:scalability:standing-certificate-cost``, ``lem:epoch:double-cover-bfs-correctness``).

**Experiment (Measurements before feasibility)** · `expt:design:measurement-gate` Before commissioning a workload profile, measure peak memory, wall time, operation counts, precision growth, evidence size and rejection behavior on the declared workload family. Record compiler, backend, platform and resource limits with the measurements. The performance risk (`warn:vision:performance`) remains held for feasibility until those measurements support a deployment budget; the semantic response to exhaustion is fixed here.

